these Syrian school children enjoy a  rare opportunity they get to have  english lessons in a school for refugees  on the edge of istanbul among them is  nine-year-old juma from Aleppo his  father was killed in the Syrian war juma  arrived in Istanbul late last year with  his mother and five siblings refugees  operate the school themselves but many  of those most in need can't attend  mungkin hueco few survive masala there  are many reasons students don't come for  some the school is too far and the  family doesn't have money for a bus for  others the father is unemployed and the  children have to work so the family can  survive and many more children have been  out of school for so long now that they  just don't keep up my sheet hath you my  you see juma lives just a short distance  from the school people donated his  clothing and school books he and his  siblings haven't received support from  the Turkish authorities nonetheless some  of his Turkish neighbors are unhappy  about the situation these refugees are  an enormous burden for our country  financially and mentally and all the  other countries have left us to deal  with it all alone eh by dashiell you  Juma's mother doesn't want to be filmed  every month she pays 100 80 euros to  rent this damp apartment with no warm  water the only place Juma can do his  homework is on the balcony but he  doesn't want to complain almost without  butter on mother sue actually I'm doing  quite well I can go to school and other  kids can't do that but of course I want  to return home to Syria soon nearly half  of the estimated two million Syrian  refugees in Turkey are minors but only a  small number of them live in the refugee  camps along the border most of the  families are scraping by in turkey's  larger cities they do have the right to  access free health care they can also  apply for a work permit as of recently  but this Syrian man who's now collecting  trash says it's not as easy as it  sounds tears are 11 professional mom  electives are but it's very difficult to  find proper work doing so requires  official documents that can take months  to get that's why a lot of us are trying  to survive on the jobs like these but  small children are often hit hardest by  having fled from home they need access  to schools already Syria is facing the  prospect of a lost generation
 

